Added a `plone-identity` driver for signing in against another Plone site running this package's `[server]` layer. Configuring one meant the generic OIDC driver plus knowing, from reading the server layer's source, that it publishes an address claim behind a scope of its own, that its `sub` is a stable opaque userid, and which of its claims map onto which member field. All of that is now the driver's own defaults.

It *is* the generic OIDC driver — a peer built on this package is a conforming OIDC provider and gets no special path through discovery, the flow or claim normalization. What the subclass adds is configuration: the `address` scope alongside the usual three, a seeded mapping covering every claim the peer releases (including `address.formatted`, the dotted path this package's property map exists for), and `subject` as the userid source, so one person keeps one id across the federation rather than each site inventing its own name for the same human. A userid already taken locally still gets a suffix, as it always did.

`BaseDriver` grew a `default_userid_source` for this, so a driver that knows something about its provider can move the default. Everything else keeps the random id, which stays the right answer for a provider a site has no particular trust in. @ericof
